  "Number": 10,
  "Question": "After rationalizing the numerator of \\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt3-\\sqrt2}{\\sqrt3},\\) the denominator in simplest form is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\sqrt3(\\sqrt3+\\sqrt2)\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\sqrt3(\\sqrt3-\\sqrt2)\\)",
    "C": "\\(3-\\sqrt3\\sqrt2\\)",
    "D": "\\(3+\\sqrt6\\)",
    "E": "None of these answers"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "To make the numerator rational, multiply by its conjugate, \\(\\sqrt3+\\sqrt2\\). This number is positive, so multiplying both numerator and denominator by it preserves the fraction:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt3-\\sqrt2}{\\sqrt3}\n=\\frac{(\\sqrt3-\\sqrt2)(\\sqrt3+\\sqrt2)}{\\sqrt3(\\sqrt3+\\sqrt2)}\n=\\frac{3-2}{3+\\sqrt6}\n=\\frac1{3+\\sqrt6}.\n\\]\nThe difference-of-squares identity removes the radicals from the numerator. Choice A gives the denominator before its multiplication is simplified; the requested simplest form is \\(\\boxed{3+\\sqrt6}\\), choice D.",
